  "concurrency_mechanism": "1 worker x 20 slots",
  "is_default": false,
  "notes": "2000 independent single-task workflow runs (no DAG, no parents), bulk-triggered with the Python SDK's run_many(wait_for_result=False) -> BulkTriggerWorkflow gRPC, which the SDK splits into 2 chunks of 1000 (its MAX_BULK_WORKFLOW_RUN_BATCH_SIZE). Pre-enqueue is real: the worker is paused through PATCH /api/v1/workers/{id} {isPaused:true} before the bulk trigger and unpaused immediately after it returns, so all 2000 rows are QUEUED before any slot is filled. All 2000 rows of a rep share one inserted_at per 1000-row chunk; the two chunks are ~0.12s apart and the first task starts within ~35ms of the insert, so submission is not inside the throughput window. Hatchet's default slot count is 100; it was set to 20 so every engine in this study runs at the same concurrency. One discarded warmup + 5 measured reps, 60s idle between reps, 90s settle after the worker connected. Timings are Hatchet's own: created_at from v1_tasks_olap.inserted_at (byte-identical to the OLTP v1_task.inserted_at, checked by join), started_at/completed_at from the STARTED and FINISHED rows in v1_task_events_olap.",
